{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_clhohriw7uljozcquqvxszii3degxog7em3ohx2jdtp26nz5rd2a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. The translation is complete and verified.\n\n## What the C library is\n\n`c_src/` is small — 3 files, one translation unit (`src/lib.c`, 35 lines) and one public header. I enumerated the full surface first as required:\n\n```\n$ nm -D --defined-only libc_src.so   →   T colourblind\n```\n\nExactly **one** public symbol. No namespace/renaming macros in `include/lib.h`; the three matrix functions (`Protanopia`, `Deuteranopia`, `Tritanopia`) are `static` and therefore not exported.\n\n## Files written (in the current dir, not `c_src/`)\n\n- **`Cargo.toml`** — `crate-type = [\"cdylib\"]`\n- **`src/lib.rs`** — `colourblind` as `#[unsafe(no_mangle)] pub unsafe extern \"C\"`, plus the three private helpers\n\n## Symbol diff — empty\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| C exports | `colourblind` |\n| Rust exports | `colourblind` |\n| C − Rust | *(nothing)* |\n\n## Bit-exactness verification\n\nI built a `dlopen`-based differential harness loading both `.so`s side by side and comparing raw **bit patterns** (not printed approximations):\n\n- **110,663,296 finite-input cases → 0 mismatches**, against C built at `-O0`, `-O2` *and* `-O3`. Coverage: 8M random full-bit-space finite floats, the complete 256×256×256 RGB grid in both `0..255` and `i/255.0` scalings × all 3 impairments, and 2M denormal/tiny-exponent cases.\n- Printed output for sample colours is byte-identical (`cmp` clean).\n- Unhandled enum values (3, 4, 5) leave `*R/*G/*B` untouched in both — the C `switch` has no `default`, and I reproduced that rather than \"fixing\" it.\n\nKey fidelity details I checked rather than assumed:\n- The C assembly uses pure `mulss`/`addss`/`subss` — **no FMA contraction** — so plain `f32` ops in Rust match. I verified this in the generated code rather than trusting `-ffp-contract` defaults.\n- Several coefficient pairs (e.g. `0.17055699213417f` vs `0.17055699092998f`) round to the *same* `f32`; GCC CSEs them. Rust's literal rounding gives the identical constants, so this is a non-issue.\n- Association is preserved as `((a*R + b*G) + c*B)`, matching C's left-to-right evaluation.\n\n## One honest caveat\n\nWith **NaN or Inf inputs**, results can differ in NaN *payload/sign*. This is not a translation defect — it is not a defined property of the C library. I confirmed the C builds disagree among themselves:\n\n- gcc `-O0` vs gcc `-O2`: **50** differing cases\n- clang `-O2` vs gcc `-O2`: **82** differing cases\n\nThe cause is x86 SSE \"first source operand wins\" NaN selection combined with the compiler freely swapping operands of commutative `addss`. GCC at `-O2` actually propagates B's NaN for `red`, contradicting the source's own left-to-right order. Since no two C builds agree, matching one would mean over-fitting to a single compiler invocation while breaking the others. Rust sits inside that same equivalence class (48 diffs vs clang). Notably, **zero finite inputs produce NaN outputs** (measured over the 110M cases), so this is unreachable except by explicitly passing NaN/Inf in."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1841,"cache_read_input_tokens":32882,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1841,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9db704a2-b30d-443a-bf5e-e75084802298","uuid":"0f569ce6-9177-43b4-903c-249d14bc8df9","timestamp":"2026-08-18T15:07:13.517Z"}
